# Hardware Architecture & Blynk IoT Setup Guide

This document supplies the comprehensive physical layout configuration, electrical power scheme, and Blynk virtual pin allocations for the IoT-enabled Smart Home Security System.

---

## Power Distribution Scheme

The system relies on a unified voltage network managed directly from the central processing chip:
1. Primary Power Link: The entire prototyping board receives its electrical supply from a 5V USB cable plugged directly into the ESP8266 development board via the Micro USB port.
2. Logic Voltage Rails: The ESP8266 breaks out 3.3V and VIN (5V) down the parallel power tracks of the breadboard to keep all sensory peripherals running.

System Protection Tip: Always install a fixed resistor in series with the LED indicator lamp. This element limits maximum current flowing through the microcontroller pin, protecting the internal LED hardware from blowing out.

---

## Detailed Pin Interconnections

### 1. PIR Motion Sensor
The Passive Infrared sensor monitors surrounding thermal variations and returns a simple high or low digital signal when movement breaks across its tracking zone.
- Connect the PIR VCC pin to the ESP8266 VIN pin (5V line) or 5V rail.
- Connect the PIR GND pin to an ESP8266 GND terminal.
- Connect the PIR Digital Output signal pin to ESP8266 GPIO 14 (labeled as D5).

### 2. Light Dependent Resistor (LDR)
The LDR changes its internal resistance based on ambient lux levels. It forms a voltage divider alongside a fixed resistor to send changing analog levels back to the board.
- Connect one terminal wire of the LDR to the ESP8266 3.3V track.
- Connect the opposite terminal wire of the LDR to the ESP8266 A0 Analog Input pin.
- Run a pull-down resistor from that same A0 junction track directly to an ESP8266 GND terminal.

### 3. LED Lighting Output
The LED provides automated nocturnal illumination whenever ambient light parameters fall past safe baseline variables.
- Connect the long positive leg (Anode) of the LED to ESP8266 GPIO 12 (labeled as D6).
- Connect the short negative leg (Cathode) of the LED to one end of your protective resistor.
- Connect the remaining open end of the resistor to an ESP8266 GND terminal.

### 4. Piezo Buzzer Intrusion Alarm
The buzzer functions as an audible sound siren whenever unexpected activity steps inside the security tracking zone during dark cycles.
- Connect the positive wire terminal of the buzzer to ESP8266 GPIO 13 (labeled as D7).
- Connect the negative wire terminal of the buzzer to an ESP8266 GND terminal.

